-### Grid Querying with Interpolation
-Querying volume density sigma and color c from feature grids along camera rays using interpolation operators [S19]. It reduces computational burden by replacing trilinear interpolation with linear/bilinear interpolation of vector/matrix factors [S19].
+### [Analytic Derivative of Tri-linear Interpolation](6919-analytic-derivative-of-tri-linear-interpolation.md)
+Computes the exact partial derivatives of a tri-linearly interpolated grid function (such as color $\vec{c}$ or density $\sigma$) with respect to the sample location $p_i = (x_i, y_i, z_i)$. It is primarily used for gradient-based optimization in neural implicit representations, such as SLAM and NeRF variants, where analytical gradients are required for backpropagation or optimization steps. Unlike numerical differentiation or interpolated gradient methods, this approach provides the exact derivative of the underlying tri-linear basis function by solving for the polynomial coefficients of the interpolation within a single voxel.
 
-### Majorant Fix for Interpolation
-Adjusts the stored maximum density value in each leaf node to account for interpolation artifacts [S20, unofficial]. When interpolating near leaf boundaries, the stencil may sample neighboring leaves with higher values, causing the leaf's stored max to be an underestimate [S20, unofficial]. This function ensures the majorant is a valid upper bound for any interpolated value within the leaf [S20, unofficial]. It iterates over all leaves, computes the interpolation Area of Effect (AoE) bounding box, and updates the leaf's max density based on neighbors [S20, unofficial]. The parameter `order` is 1 for trilinear interpolation [S20, unofficial].
+### [Ghost Cell Stitching for Node-Centered AMR](8949-ghost-cell-stitching-for-node-centered-amr.md)
+A method to handle stitching between different AMR levels for node-centered data by introducing ghost cells.
+
+### [Smoothstep Trilinear Interpolation](9344-smoothstep-trilinear-interpolation.md)
+A technique used in multiresolution hash encodings to achieve $C^1$-smooth interpolation (continuous first derivatives) in the spatial domain. It solves the problem of derivative discontinuities at grid boundaries inherent in standard trilinear interpolation, which can cause artifacts in neural rendering and gradient-based optimization. It sets itself apart from higher-order polynomial interpolations (like bicubic) by achieving similar smoothness properties at effectively zero computational cost, as the overhead is hidden by memory bottlenecks. It builds on [3D Trilinear Interpolation](2613-3d-trilinear-interpolation.md) by modifying the interpolation weights rather than the data structure or lookup count.
+
+### [Barycentric Interpolation](9603-barycentric-interpolation.md)
+Interpolates learnable feature vectors at the $n+1$ vertices of a simplex cell using barycentric coordinates derived from sorted, skewed coordinates. It serves as an efficient alternative to computationally expensive $n$-linear interpolation (such as trilinear interpolation) by maintaining similarity to standard interpolation schemes while avoiding the kernel summation overhead found in simplex noise algorithms. It is specifically designed for simplex-based structures where points are mapped to vertices via coordinate skewing and simplicial subdivision.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per query point, involving a constant number of arithmetic operations (18 multiplications and 14 additions in the expanded form, or fewer in the recursive form) [S4, unofficial][S6, unofficial][S8, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_x N_y N_z)$ for the dense grid storage [S1][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Rendering rectilinear grids with trilinear interpolation is faster than curvilinear volumes, where precalculation of coefficients can take ~110 seconds and intersection testing dominates [S1]. Trilinear interpolation uses 8 neighbor points, whereas tricubic interpolation uses 64 points [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, computationally efficient, provides smoother results than nearest-neighbor [S4, unofficial][S5, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** $C^0$ continuity only; artifacts (diamonds) at high magnification [S4, unofficial]; not suitable for irregular or rotated grids without modification [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** Standard volume rendering where performance is critical and high-frequency detail is not required [G].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When $C^1$ continuity is required (use cubic or higher-order methods) or when dealing with highly irregular/curvilinear grids [S1][S4, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per sample point, involving 7 multiplications and 12 additions/subtractions for the efficient separable implementation [S16, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory, requiring storage for only the 8 corner values and fractional weights [S12, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Trilinear interpolation is significantly slower than nearest-neighbor but faster than cubic interpolation [S9, unofficial]. In curvilinear volumes, precalculating trilinear coefficients can take ~110 seconds, but rendering remains dominated by ray intersection testing [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Provides smooth, continuous variation between sample points, superior to nearest-neighbor [S17, unofficial]. It is the standard filter for structured rectilinear data [S11, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Only $C^0$ continuous; discontinuities in derivatives can cause artifacts in gradient-based optimization [S2]. Artifacts like "3D crosses" appear at high magnification [S9, unofficial]. Not suitable for irregular or rotated cells without significant modification [S2].
+*   **When to Use:** Standard volume rendering on regular grids where smoothness is desired but higher-order continuity is not critical [S3, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When $C^1$ continuity is required (use [Smoothstep Trilinear Interpolation](9344-smoothstep-trilinear-interpolation.md) or [Cubic Volume Interpolation](4278-cubic-volume-interpolation.md)), or when dealing with irregular/unstructured grids (use [Barycentric Interpolation](9603-barycentric-interpolation.md)) [S2][S8, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Trilinear interpolation was developed as a standard method for volume rendering of rectilinear and curvilinear grids [S1]. It is a fundamental technique in computer graphics for sampling 3D textures and volume data [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial][S4, unofficial][S5, unofficial][S6, unofficial][S7, unofficial][S8, unofficial][S9, unofficial]. The method assumes the underlying function varies linearly along lines parallel to the cartesian axes [S1]. It is widely used in direct volume rendering (DVR) and is a component of more complex methods like [Trilinear Gradient Interpolation](3470-trilinear-gradient-interpolation.md) and [Cubic Volume Interpolation](4278-cubic-volume-interpolation.md) [S1][S3, unofficial][S4, unofficial][S11, unofficial].
+Trilinear interpolation was introduced as a standard method for reconstructing values in regular, rectilinear, and curvilinear data sets for volume rendering [S1]. It is widely used in direct volume rendering (DVR) ray casting pipelines, where sample points between voxels require interpolation to determine density and color [S3, S4, unofficial]. The method assumes the underlying function varies linearly along axes parallel to the cartesian axes [S2]. It is often contrasted with nearest-neighbor (box) and cubic spline filters [S3, unofficial].
 
